ws  you know but we're working on it yeah no  no no  so where where did these kind of twin  loves come of course you know  everyone knows by now shirley knows by  now about the phd in astrophysics but  but but more than that i mean there has  been a lifelong fascination hasn't that  same as myself i i cannot stop myself  from from from gazing up  yeah well it came from patrick moore i  think my dear friend patrick moore who  became a kind of adopted uncle to me i  used to beg my parents to be allowed to  stay up for as late as 10 o'clock to see  the sky at night and he presented that  show for 50 years which is a world  record  every time that music came on you see it  already it's linked  that stirring wonderful music that made  you feel like you were looking into the  cosmos i was hooked and i wanted to be  an astronomer and i wanted to be a  musician as well i actually wrote to  patrick  when i was a kid i must have been about  nine years old and what i asked him was  what is the music that your program  starts with and he wrote back on his  typewriter personally and said it's pele  arsene medicine by sibelius  at the castle gate and i rushed out and  bought the record i still have it and  it's been a great inspiration to me  music and astronomy  always do seem together to go together  it seems to me most of the famous  astronomers in history have been  musicians too including including isaac  newton  and we run a thing called starmus my  dear friend garrick israelian the  eminent armenian astronomer runs starmus  every year or a couple of years and it's  a combination of astrophysics  astronautics and music and it just it's  a rocket it's something great everybody  smiles the whole time well i'm i i  sincerely wish you very very well with  it enjoy yourself tonight you're back on  tour a little later this year i spoke to  adam lambert last year he's a star  anyway brian may and all your followers  on the internet thanks very much for  joining us really appreciat